   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "From the country to the person",
     "texto": [
      "To talk about origin and nationality, we use the verb **ser** with adjectives or with the preposition **de**:\n\n- Adjectives ending in **-o** change to **-a** in the feminine: *italiano / italiana*, *chino / china*.\n- Adjectives ending in a consonant add **-a** and drop the written accent: *francés / francesa*, *alemán / alemana*, *japonés / japonesa*.\n- Some adjectives have only one form for masculine and feminine: *estadounidense*, *marroquí*.\n\nCountry names always start with a capital letter (*Francia*, *Japón*). In contrast, nationalities and languages are always written with lowercase letters (*francés*, *japonés*).\n\nMany nationality adjectives also name the language itself (*el francés*, *el chino*). Remember that after the verb **hablar**, the definite article is omitted: we say **hablo francés**."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Nationalities in masculine and feminine",
     "texto": [
      "Notice the gender agreement with the subject pronoun:\n\n- **Masculine**: Pablo es español.\n- **Feminine**: Ana es española.\n\nNotice how adjectives with a final accent on -és or -án drop the written accent when adding the feminine -a (alemán → alemana, francés → francesa)."
     ],
     "ejemplos": [
      {
       "quien": {
        "texto": "",
        "lengua": "alumno"
       },
       "estudio": "Pablo es español.",
       "alumno": "Pablo is Spanish.",
       "literal": "",
       "estructura": "",
       "nota": [],
       "analisis": [
        [
         "Pablo",
         "subject"
        ],
        [
         "es español",
         "predicate"
